1
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,620
What are you doing?

2
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:17,060
I think I've got them.

3
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:19,800
You think you've got what?

4
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,940
Performing arts--performing arts centers.

5
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:33,090
I think I've got them all, but I'm not sure.

6
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:39,270
I think you have got it, Richard.

7
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:47,060
Lincoln Center, home of the Metropolitan Opera,

8
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:58,050
the New York City Ballet, the New York Philharmonic.

9
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:02,180
Shubert Alley, center of the theater on Broadway. Shubert Alley

10
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:06,060
And Carnegie Hall.

11
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:12,810
And the Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in Washington.

12
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:19,950
And the Music Center in Los Angeles.

13
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:28,320
And the others. I do have it all.

14
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:33,280
You have been working on this for some time, Richard.

15
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,740
I'm glad you feel you've finally put it all together.

16
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:39,190
What now?

17
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:42,300
Now for the hard part.

18
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:44,620
Oh, you think you're going to have a hard time

19
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,710
getting the album published?

20
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:50,350
Well, it won't be easy.

21
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:55,530
So, what do you think you're going to do?

22
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:59,270
Does the name Harvey Carlson ring a bell?

23
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:02,460
Oh, yes! Harvey Carlson! I remember.

24
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,250
He said I should call him.

25
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:07,510
He's the publisher of the Carlson Publishing Company.

26
00:02:07,510 --> 00:02:09,370
He said they need a new book of photographs,

27
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,550
and he really liked my concept.

28
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:18,200
So, why don't you call him in the morning?

29
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,670
Do you think I have enough to show him?

30
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:23,840
I've been through every section with you, Richard.

31
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:25,870
It's quite complete.

32
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:28,560
And now that you're satisfied with the performing arts section,

33
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,070
I think you should show it to Mr. Carlson.

34
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:37,160
You're right. My Family Album, U.S.A. feels right.

35
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:41,870
I'll call in the morning and set up an appointment to see him.

36
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:47,120
He's going to love your work.

37
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:54,130
Come on. Let's get some sleep.

38
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:58,650
Is it too early to call Mr. Carlson?

39
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:04,380
Seven after nine? No. I'm sure he's in his office.

40
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:10,910
His number is five, five, five, seven, five, three, two.

41
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:19,000
Five, five, five, seven, five, three, two. 5557532

42
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,510
Hello. Mr. Carlson, please.

43
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:25,830
Mr. Carlson is busy at the moment.

44
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:28,210
I'd like to make an appointment with him.

45
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:29,550
And your name is ...?

46
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:31,100
My name is Richard Stewart.

47
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,270
He told me to call him about my project,

48
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:35,030
Family Album, U.S.A.

49
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:45,000
One moment, please.

50
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,720
What's going on?

51
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:51,340
I guess they're trying to set up an appointment for me.

52
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:54,550
Mr. Stewart, I just spoke to Mr. Carlson.

53
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:55,950
He would like to see you.

54
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:58,170
But the only time he's available this week

55
00:03:58,170 --> 00:04:00,130
is tomorrow morning at ten o'clock.

56
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:06,480
Thanks. Thanks a lot. Good-bye.

57
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:10,970
Well, it's done. Tomorrow morning at a publisher's office.

58
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,970
Well, finally a publisher will see my work.
